===========================================================================
VERDICT for 28126 / 28127 / 28128 / 28129: UNMEASURED
  The guest-VLAN MECHANISM is proven. The traffic claims the cases actually
  make cannot be measured on this bench.
===========================================================================

PROVEN
  `auth guest-vlan 200` on port2.0.2 moves an unauthenticated port into the
  guest VLAN -- `show vlan 200` reports:
      200  VLAN0200  STATIC  ACTIVE  port2.0.2(u)
  i.e. the port really is placed in the guest VLAN, untagged, while no
  supplicant is authorised ("No supplicants", authorizedSupplicantNum 0).
  `show platform counter sdma` -- the command all four cases call for -- IS
  supported on this platform and returns per-stack-member register dumps.

NOT MEASURABLE HERE, AND WHY
 1. All four cases require "L3 traffic sent BETWEEN SUPPLICANTS" -- two hosts
    both in the guest VLAN, talking to each other through the DUT. This bench
    has ONE usable supplicant port. Each IE520-28GSX member has exactly one
    copper port: port1.0.2 is the capture egress (to the x230), port2.0.2 is
    the supplicant, port3.0.2 has no cable, port4.0.2 is the LAG to the 4050.
    A second cabled port in the guest VLAN is a recable.
    (The 0/10 transit figures above are NOT a product result -- they are the
    guest VLAN correctly isolating port2.0.2 from the VLAN 1 capture path.)

 2. The distinguishing claim in each pair is a RATE claim:
      28126/28128 hw forwarding disabled -> "should show loss ... bottleneck
                  by cpu throughput"
      28127/28129 hw forwarding enabled  -> "NO loss ... achieves line rate,
                  all sdma counters zero"
    Separating "CPU-bottlenecked with loss" from "line rate, no loss" needs a
    line-rate generator. The case text names ixia. This bench injects with
    scapy at ~10 pps, which both configurations forward without loss, so the
    measurement cannot discriminate between them at all.

TO CLOSE THESE FOUR: a second cabled port in the guest VLAN, and a traffic
source capable of line rate. Both are bench-access items for Terrence.
